facts:
A function is **one-to-one** when it passes the **horizontal line test**: every horizontal line $y = c$ cuts the curve at most once.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.9,0) -- (2.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-2.6,1.5) -- (2.6,1.5);
\node[above right] at (-2.6,1.5) {$y = c$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.2:2.2, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.3*\x*\x*\x});
\fill (1.71,1.5) circle (2pt);
\node at (-1.9,2.7) {$y = f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A curve that turns around fails the test: $y = c$ cuts it at two points $(x_1, c)$ and $(x_2, c)$, so it is **many-to-one**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.9,0) -- (2.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-2.6,1.5) -- (2.6,1.5);
\node[above right] at (-2.6,1.5) {$y = c$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.4:2.4, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {3*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\fill (-1.18,1.5) circle (2pt);
\fill (1.18,1.5) circle (2pt);
\node at (2.0,2.6) {$y = f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

