The graph of $y = x^3 - 3x$ is shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.14:2.14, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x*\x - 3*\x});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
State whether this graph represents a one-to-one function, and justify using a geometric test.

The graph of $y = 2^x$ is shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.8:2.2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {exp(\x*ln(2))});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Determine whether $y = 2^x$ is a one-to-one function.

